The main obstacle to implementing your ideas is that the large amount
of work needed to get the kind of graphics you want into the game, and
client/server operation, and any number of other possible large-scale
projects with the game, will much more likely happen with a dedicated
team.  But the whole paradigm of "official" nethack development, for a
long time now, has been to code glacially slowly, in secret, and most
of all extremely conservatively.

That sets the tone for the rest of development on the game, because
putting together committed people for substantive projects is not a
trivial task, and the best recruiting ground for such people -
r.g.r.n. - is infused with sympathizers, or even apologists, for "the
Devteam"'s approach to development, which tends to strangle large,
active, concerted efforts in the cradle with a lot of naysaying.  As a
result almost all work done on the game comes in the form of piecemail
patches contributed by aficionados, and therefore without synthesis
and wide adoption rate.

If we had an official development team that was actually active,
show-me-the-money active, with a non-moribund rate of coding and
release, the environment would be dynamic and help spur more
development such as yours.

